<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amirids</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 978–<a href="/years/1009">1009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Amirid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Amirids were the family of al-Mansur (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>), the chief minister who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> from the 970s. His sons Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo</a> succeeded him until Sanchuelo's fall in 1009 set off the civil war that destroyed the caliphate. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> and a grandson of al-Mansur then founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amirids.jpg" alt="Amirids" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AbdurRahman AbdulMoneim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amirid_Saqaliba.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
